(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-hent-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"HENT (Head, Ears, Nose, Throat) Examination — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"During scalp inspection, which findings should be noted, similar to the skin exam?","choices":["Only skull circumference","Only hair color","Only sensation to light touch","Lesions and dandruff"],"answer":3,"exp":"The scalp is inspected for lesions and dandruff, similar to a general skin exam. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"During hair inspection, which of the following should be assessed?","choices":["Only the overall length of the scalp hair","Only the surface temperature of the scalp","Color, distribution, texture, hair loss, and nits","Only the presence of gray or white hair"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the specific hair inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the recommended general approach when examining the head, ears, nose, and throat region?","choices":["Rely on inspection alone, since palpation adds no useful information in this particular region","Move through the entire exam as quickly as possible, to minimize any patient discomfort","Avoid causing any discomfort under any circumstance, even if that limits exam completeness","Be thorough, systematic, and gentle; use look, smell, listen, and feel, without fearing necessary discomfort"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture's general approach emphasizes thoroughness, system, gentleness, using multiple senses, and not avoiding necessary discomfort. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"On otoscopic ear canal inspection, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Patency, redness, swelling, cerumen, and discharge","Only structural deformities of the outer auricle","Only the color of the tympanic membrane itself","Only swelling overlying the mastoid process"],"answer":0,"exp":"These are the specific ear canal inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"When palpating the ear, what should the examiner specifically do?","choices":["Gently move the auricle and ask about pain, then press on the tragus and mastoid","Only inspect the ear visually, with no palpation performed at any point","Only assess hearing acuity by whisper testing, with no palpation performed","Only attempt to palpate the tympanic membrane directly through the ear canal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Ear palpation includes gentle auricle movement with a pain inquiry, plus pressing on the tragus and mastoid. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"On external auricle inspection, which findings should be noted?","choices":["Discharge, lesions, redness, swelling, and deformities","Only perforation of the tympanic membrane itself","Only the color and consistency of any visible cerumen","Only the patient's formal audiometric hearing threshold"],"answer":0,"exp":"These are the specific auricle inspection findings listed prior to using the otoscope. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"When selecting an otoscope speculum for ear examination, what is the recommended approach?","choices":["Use a speculum one size larger than what fits, forcing insertion","Always use the smallest available speculum regardless of canal size","Use the largest speculum that can be easily inserted into the canal","Speculum size does not matter for examination quality"],"answer":2,"exp":"The largest speculum that fits comfortably provides the best view and seal for otoscopic examination. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"In the Weber test, where is the vibrating tuning fork placed, and what is the normal finding?","choices":["Near the ear canal only; normal is no sound heard at all","On top of the head; normal is that the tone is heard midline","On the sternum; normal is a symmetric vibration felt bilaterally","On the mastoid bone; normal is tone heard longer through air"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Weber test places the tuning fork on the vertex of the skull, and a normal result is the tone heard equally/midline in both ears. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"In an abnormal Weber test, lateralization to the affected ear suggests which type of hearing loss?","choices":["Neither type produces lateralization","Central auditory processing disorder exclusively","Sensorineural hearing loss","Conductive hearing loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"In conductive hearing loss, the Weber test lateralizes (sound is heard louder) in the affected ear. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"In the Rinne test, what does a normal result show, and what does an abnormal (conductive loss) result show?","choices":["Normal: sound heard longer through air than bone. Abnormal (conductive loss): sound heard as long or longer through bone","The Rinne test only assesses sensorineural hearing loss, and provides no information about conductive loss","Normal and abnormal results are identical and indistinguishable from each other in this test","Normal: sound heard longer through bone than air. Abnormal: sound heard longer through air than bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normally air conduction outlasts bone conduction; in conductive hearing loss, bone conduction is heard as long or longer than air conduction. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"When palpating the maxillary sinuses, what technique is used?","choices":["Only auscultate, do not palpate the maxillary sinuses","Press down and laterally away from the face","Press up over the maxillary region","Press directly into the nares"],"answer":2,"exp":"Maxillary sinus palpation involves pressing upward over the maxillary area, similar in direction to the frontal sinus technique. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"During otoscopic nasal exam, why should the examiner specifically avoid contacting the septum with the speculum?","choices":["There is no specific reason; it is simply an arbitrary technique preference","Because the septum is not a structure typically visualized during this exam","To prevent patient discomfort and potential trauma/bleeding from septal contact","Because septal contact would interfere with sinus transillumination results"],"answer":2,"exp":"The septum is sensitive and vascular; the technique specifically instructs avoiding it to prevent discomfort or trauma. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"What type of exudate characteristics should be documented during nasal mucosa inspection?","choices":["Only whether exudate is present or absent, with no further description","Exudate character is not assessed during the nasal exam","Clear, mucopurulent, or purulent","Only bloody versus non-bloody"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture specifically lists these three exudate character descriptions to note during nasal inspection. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"During nasal inspection, what should the examiner assess regarding the nares and turbinates specifically?","choices":["Only their overall color, with essentially no attention paid to their underlying structural anatomy","Their general appearance as part of the broader inspection alongside deformities, asymmetry, and polyps","Only whether the patient is able to breathe freely through each nostril one at a time","Nares and turbinates are not routinely assessed at any point during a nasal inspection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Nares and turbinates are inspected together with deformities, asymmetry, and polyps as part of the comprehensive nasal inspection. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"When inspecting the lips, which findings should be noted?","choices":["Color, moisture, lumps, ulcers, cracks, and scale","Only the patient's ability to purse the lips","Only the presence of visible dental caries","Only the symmetry of the patient's smile"],"answer":0,"exp":"These are the specific lip inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"When inspecting the gums and interdental papillae, what findings should be assessed?","choices":["Only the number of missing or absent teeth","Only the character of any tongue coating","Only the character of the patient's breath odor","Color, hypertrophy, swelling, bleeding, and recession"],"answer":3,"exp":"These are the specific gum/interdental papillae inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"How should the tongue be palpated during the oral exam?","choices":["Use a wooden tongue blade to palpate the tongue, never the gloved fingers directly","Wearing gloves, grasp the tongue with gauze to inspect it, then palpate the lateral margins","Only inspect the tongue visually, since palpation is never performed during this exam","Palpate the tongue directly with a bare, ungloved hand, without using any gauze"],"answer":1,"exp":"Gloved palpation with gauze grasping of the tongue allows inspection and palpation of the lateral margins, which is important since this is a common site for oral lesions. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"When inspecting the teeth, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Only the alignment and occlusion of the patient's bite","Only the presence or character of any tongue coating","Number, dentures/plates, decay, and discoloration/caries","Only the color of the surrounding gingival gum tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the specific teeth inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"When inspecting the hard palate, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Only whether teeth are present","Only the rate of salivary flow","Color, architecture, and lesions","Only how mobile the tongue is"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the specific hard palate inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"When inspecting the tongue, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Symmetry, deviations, color, texture, and coatings","Only the patient's ability to whistle on command","Only the visible presence and density of taste buds","Only the presence of tongue piercings, if any are seen"],"answer":0,"exp":"These are the specific tongue inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"What physical exam approach sequence (IPalPerA vs IAPerPal) is used for the HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) region compared to the abdomen?","choices":["The order of exam steps does not matter for any body region at all, including both the HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) exam and the abdominal exam","HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) exam skips percussion and auscultation entirely, relying only on inspection and palpation of the visible structures","HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) uses Inspection, Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation (IPalPerA), unlike the abdomen which uses Inspection, Auscultation, Percussion, Palpation (IAPerPal)","Both the HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) region and the abdomen use the identical Inspection, Auscultation, Percussion, Palpation (IAPerPal) sequence"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture specifies that all areas except the abdomen use IPalPerA, while the abdomen specifically uses IAPerPal (auscultation before palpation/percussion to avoid altering bowel sounds). — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"What lighting technique is helpful when inspecting the location of the thyroid gland?","choices":["Only direct overhead fluorescent lighting","No specific lighting technique is needed","Complete darkness with a handheld light source","Tangential lighting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Tangential lighting can help highlight subtle contour changes and improve visualization of thyroid location/symmetry. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 61"},{"q":"During neck inspection, which glandular structures should be assessed for enlargement?","choices":["Only the pineal gland, deep in the brain","Only the paired adrenal glands","Only the anterior thymus gland","Parotid and submandibular glands"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture specifically lists parotid and submandibular gland enlargement as part of neck inspection. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"What should the trachea's position normally be on inspection and palpation?","choices":["Midline, with symmetrical distance to the sternocleidomastoid muscles bilaterally","Position varies randomly from patient to patient with no normal reference point","Deviated slightly to the right of midline in all normal, healthy patients","Deviated slightly to the left of midline in all normal, healthy patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"A normal trachea should sit midline with a symmetrical distance to the SCM (sternocleidomastoid) muscles on both sides. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 61"},{"q":"Is a normal thyroid gland typically palpable on physical exam?","choices":["Palpability depends entirely on the patient's age, not gland size","No, a normal thyroid is often not palpable","Yes, a normal thyroid is always easily palpable in every patient","Only the isthmus is ever palpable, never the lobes, even when enlarged"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lecture states that a normal thyroid gland is often not palpable on physical exam. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"In the posterior thyroid exam, how is the left lobe palpated?","choices":["Palpate the left lobe with the left hand while displacing the trachea to the left using the right hand","Palpate the left lobe with the right hand while displacing the trachea to the right instead","Stand facing the patient directly, rather than standing behind them, for this approach","Use only a single hand throughout the entire posterior thyroid examination technique"],"answer":0,"exp":"This is the correct posterior technique for the left lobe: left hand palpates while the right hand displaces the trachea leftward. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"What overall purpose does thyroid palpation serve after inspection has been completed?","choices":["To assess for the presence of carotid bruits, using the stethoscope bell over the neck","To assess the patient's overall cervical range of motion in all directions of movement","To confirm the gland's outline, symmetry, and identify any nodules seen or suspected on inspection","To indirectly estimate the patient's blood pressure through neck vessel palpation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Palpation (both anterior and posterior techniques) is used to confirm and further characterize the thyroid outline, symmetry, and any nodules. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 62"},{"q":"When palpating the anterior superficial, deep cervical, and posterior cervical chains, what positioning maneuver helps relax the tissue?","choices":["Flex the patient's neck slightly forward toward the side being examined","Rotate the patient's head fully away with no forward flexion at all","No positioning maneuver at all is needed for these particular chains","Fully extend the patient's neck backward, away from the chest wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Slight forward neck flexion toward the examined side relaxes the overlying tissue, facilitating palpation of these chains. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 66"},{"q":"How is the deep cervical chain palpated, given that it can be uncomfortable for the patient?","choices":["Ask the patient to look straight ahead without any head movement at all, then press firmly and directly along the full length of the muscle belly, comparing both sides simultaneously","Hook fingers under the anterior sternocleidomastoid muscle, have the patient flex the neck and angle the top of the head toward the side being examined with the chin pointing away, then move the muscle backward and palpate","Use a wooden tongue blade to gently displace the sternocleidomastoid muscle aside first, then attempt to palpate the underlying tissue directly through the muscle without hooking under it","Press directly down on top of the sternocleidomastoid muscle with flat fingers, without ever hooking the fingertips underneath it or asking the patient to flex the neck"],"answer":1,"exp":"This is the specific technique described for accessing the deep cervical chain beneath the SCM (sternocleidomastoid). — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which of the following is one of the 10 cervical lymph node groups listed in the lecture?","choices":["Inguinal nodes","Axillary nodes","Popliteal nodes","Supraclavicular"],"answer":3,"exp":"Supraclavicular is one of the 10 listed cervical lymph node groups, along with occipital, pre-auricular, posterior auricular, tonsillar, submandibular, submental, superficial cervical, posterior cervical, and deep cervical. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 64"}]};
